 "transcript": [
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Our Henry has gone a bit weak and sounds rough, sort of rattly growl instead of the normal hum. It's about 12 years old. Bags and filter are new. Worth fixing?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "At twelve years old with a rattling sound indicating internal mechanical damage, it is generally not worth repairing. The cost of replacing the motor or fan assembly usually exceeds the value of an appliance of this age. From what you have described, the most likely cause is the motor bearing or fan. A whistle or a change in pitch usually means a part-blocked airway. With it switched off, look through the hose, wand and floorhead neck and check the bin inlet. Did you find a blockage and clear it, is there one you can't shift, is the hose split, or was it all clear?\n[Helpful to see: image \"Poor suction — what to check first\" — Lost suction is usually a full bin, a dirty filter or a blockage in the hose, tube or floor head — check and clear those before suspecting the motor. Switch off and unplug first. Typical layout — your model may differ.]"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Thank you. I dropped a coin through the hose and it went straight through, so there's no blockage there. The suction is weak at both the hose end and at the head, and the bag and filter were fitted correctly last week."
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Check the bin clicks fully home and each filter is seated properly (and dry) — a gap makes it whistle. Was the bin or a filter not seated or clogged (and is it sorted), is a filter damaged, or was everything seated and clean?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Yes, everything was seated properly and clean. The bag is a genuine HepaFlo and the cloth filter is new. I should also mention that after about five minutes it gives off a hot, burning electrical smell. When I looked at the exhaust grille on top in the dim hall, I also saw sparks flickering inside."
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "⚠ Stop using this appliance\nA burning or hot-plastic smell, smoke, scorching or melting can mean an electrical fault or a fire risk. Stop using it, switch it off and unplug it (or turn it off at the fuse box), and don't run it again — not even as a test — until it has been checked by a qualified engineer. The checks point to the motor (bearings / brushes / winding). To find the right part, could you give me the make and model number? It is usually on the body, often under the bin or battery.\n[Panel: \"To check a suitable replacement — Enter your model number below. Once we have the model I can check whether a suitable replacement is available. It's usually on a sticker inside the door or around the frame, or on the back of the appliance.\"]"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Thank you, I'll switch it off and leave it unplugged. It's a Numatic Henry, and the label on the bottom says HVR 200-22."
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "⚠ Stop using this appliance\nFrom what you have checked, this needs an appliance engineer to look at it: the next steps are beyond the checks that are safe to do yourself."
  }
 ],
